## 5. CRITICAL REQUIREMENT: OFFLINE-FIRST OPERATION

Implement the offline workflow shown in the attached diagram.

NO INTERNET
    ↓
OPEN CACHED FUELRESCUE APPLICATION
    ↓
READ AVAILABLE LOCAL DATA
    ↓
CREATE EMERGENCY REQUEST
    ↓
GENERATE UNIQUE CLIENT REQUEST ID
    ↓
SAVE REQUEST IN INDEXEDDB
    ↓
MARK AS "PENDING SYNC"
    ↓
INTERNET BECOMES AVAILABLE
    ↓
TRIGGER SYNCHRONIZATION
    ↓
SEND PENDING REQUEST TO SPRING BOOT API
    ↓
SERVER VALIDATES REQUEST
    ↓
SERVER STORES REQUEST IN AIVEN POSTGRESQL
    ↓
RETURN SERVER REQUEST ID AND CONFIRMED STATUS
    ↓
UPDATE LOCAL RECORD
    ↓
MARK AS "SYNCED"

### Offline implementation requirements

1. Cache the application shell and essential static assets with a Service Worker so the previously loaded application can reopen offline.
2. Save new requests in IndexedDB immediately, without waiting for the backend.
3. Generate a UUID client_request_id for every new request.
4. Assign PENDING_SYNC locally when no server confirmation exists.
5. Display a visible offline banner and pending-sync indicator.
6. Retry synchronization when connectivity returns, when the application resumes, and when the user manually requests synchronization.
7. Use browser Background Sync where supported, with foreground retry fallbacks for browsers that do not support it.
8. Synchronize queued records in a controlled sequence and preserve failed records for retry.
9. Use exponential backoff for transient failures.
10. Make server request creation idempotent using the client request ID and an appropriate database uniqueness constraint.
11. If the same request is retried multiple times, return the existing result rather than creating duplicates.
12. Validate every request on the backend before accepting it.
13. Update the local record only after receiving a successful server response.
14. Handle authentication expiry, validation errors, network timeouts, server failures, and conflicting updates.
15. Maintain a retry count, last synchronization attempt, last error, and synchronization timestamp.
16. Provide a manual “Sync Now” action.
17. Show distinct states: Saved Offline, Pending Sync, Syncing, Synced, and Sync Failed.
18. Preserve user-entered information across application restarts.
19. Keep local and server IDs separate.
20. Use database transactions for server-side request creation and related updates.

### Essential safety limitation

Offline request creation is possible, but real-time provider matching, dispatch, remote notifications, current outlet availability, and live route information require connectivity and relevant services.

Clearly communicate that an offline request is saved locally and has NOT yet been transmitted to an assistance provider.

Do not claim that fuel is on its way until a provider has actually accepted and confirmed the request.

